    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass (the acceptance suite is the project's only test class).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added an optional `postcode` field to owners, validated against the city's region when present, and stored/returned when given \u2014 keeping the create contract backward-compatible.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added `postcode` (optional string, `^[0-9]{4}$`) to the `OwnerFields` schema, so the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` gain the field (and MapStruct maps it both ways automatically).\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` column, getter, and setter to the JPA entity.\n\n3. **`model/Postcode.java`** (new) \u2014 Helper that checks a postcode is exactly 4 digits and, when the city maps to a known region (via existing `Locality`), falls within the fixed range: NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099. A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode.\n\n4. **`function/owner/ValidateOwnerFields.java`** (POST pipeline) \u2014 Added `validatePostcode`: when a postcode is present it must pass `Postcode.isValidForCity`, otherwise a `\"postcode\"` error is added \u2192 400 via the existing `OwnerFieldsInvalidException` handler. Absent/blank postcode is skipped (optional).\n\n5. **`function/owner/ApplyOwner.java`** (PUT pipeline) \u2014 Persists postcode on update.\n\n6. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 Added the `postcode` column so the JPA entity maps cleanly (the test profile uses hsqldb).\n\nThe validation runs in `ValidateOwnerFields` (the first POST step, before persistence), matching the project's \"validate before load/save\" convention so an out-of-range or malformed postcode is a 400 rather than a persisted record.",
